        "finalMessage": "I fixed all six issues in `serialize.ts`. I couldn't confirm it with a re-lint: oxlint isn't installed here, and the `tsc` type-check needed approval that a non-interactive run can't get.\n\n**What changed**\n- **Issues 1, 3 and 5** (`serializeArray`, `serializeObject` and `convertToSerializedForm`): these three call each other in a loop. Turning them into top-level `const` arrow functions would always leave one used before it's defined, whatever the order. So they're now methods on one `serializer` object and call each other through `serializer.…`. Those calls sit inside method bodies, so they only run after the object exists. That clears issues 2 and 4 as well.\n- **Issues 2 and 4**: the two early calls to `convertToSerializedForm` are now `serializer.convertToSerializedForm(...)`.\n- **Issue 6**: `export function serializeProps` is now `export const serializeProps = (...) => {...}`, and it calls `serializer.serializeObject`.\n\n**Behavior** is unchanged. The function bodies, default parameters, the cycle check and the error messages are all the same. The methods never use `this`, so calling them through the object makes no difference. `serializeProps` is still exported under the same name.\n\n**Other details:**\n- The object's keys are in alphabetical order. The file looks like it was run through `sort-keys` (see `PROP_TYPE`), so this avoids a new warning.\n- The multi-line error strings are left exactly as they were, including their unindented continuation lines.\n- I added a two-line comment explaining why the object exists.",
